ecisions closer to  home and there's something quite wrong  about the british constitution now  that we have a multinational state we  have regions with a great deal of  different needs and different traditions  and different cultures and different  desires  but this government has not found a way  of cooperating with them of consulting  them of working with them  and there seems to be no formal  mechanisms in place by which the mayors  or the first ministers  can actually talk to the prime minister  or talk to the leading cabinet ministers  and this really matters because we  failed on test and track as far as i can  see  we were too slow on testing there's a  huge standoff about the local lockdowns  we've got a huge issue coming up on  vaccination where  it's great what the scientists have done  but unless we have local systems of  delivery that are coordinated  with the training of people and the  supply and of course the  the logistics to get supplies there then  that vaccination thing will not work the  way it should and it's a huge task ahead  of us  and so the british way of governing has  got to change if we're going to meet the  needs and aspirations of people  in all parts of the country at the same  time though  is there an argument that further  devolution does  put you further along the path to  independence you wrote to the new  statements when recently  it was naive to think we could create  strong scottish welsh  northern irish and regional  decision-making bodies  and automatically expect people to feel  more british as a result  equally it was naive not to anticipate  that devolution  could create a megaphone for  intensifying resentment  so do you think the last labour  government wasn't  well the word there is automatic we  should not have expected that  automatically everything would be okay  you've got to build  systems where you cooperate between the  different administrations  where there's some form of coordination  and collaboration  where 