ign to stay  you're going to lose aren't you  i think boris johnson has got to do  something  to show that he cares about scotland but  he's also going to do it for wales  and he's also going to do it for the  regions of england where quite frankly  some of his statements have gone down  badly badly as well you know as a prime  minister  and i found this you've got to spend  time bringing people together  in the end it's you the prime minister  that's got to try to unite the country  you can't rely  on different parts of the country coming  together automatically  you've got to bring people together and  he has no forum in which he consults the  people who are making decisions about  this  virus in the regions at no permanent  forum  there is no constitutional working  relationship that is actually  operational between him and scotland  wales and northern ireland  and he's really got to think about that  and i don't want it to be an issue of  personalities  i want it to be about how we approach  the future of the united kingdom because  people are really fed up a lot of people  feel that they are treated as  second-class  citizens they feel that some of the  decisions over the lockdowns were made  without taking into account the needs of  the north in particular  and i really think we've got to face up  to this you know as a prime minister  you've also got to be two steps ahead of  events  and this is an issue that's coming down  the tracks yes we've got to get  vaccination out there and i do applaud  the scientists that have made this  possible  yes we've got to get the economy back  working again but to do these things  properly in the future to manage public  health and to manage the economy better  we've got to have a better way of  working together we've got to have a  better way of showing that  joint working can bring better results  yeah absolutely critical as you said  in that fight against covid um i'm keen  to get your  opinion on brexit too because it does  feel as if those talks are reaching  an absolutely critical phase uh this  weekend if boris johnson does come back  with a trade deal  should labour back it  this is very interesting by the way  because he always takes it to the last  minute  and he's always hoping that angela  merkel or someone  will step in so the irish tea shop  stepped in the last time and they made a  deal  i think he's hoping that angela merkel  who's really chairing the european  council  next thursday is going to step in and i  don't think you can say what you do  about the deal until you know what the  deal is  but what's actually happening is if  there is no deal now  i see huge international implications  from what he fails to deliver  because we would be in an economic war  with europe that would cost us  very dearly food drugs and everything  else we'd find it difficult to get into  the country without tariffs and without  holdups but we'd also be in an economic  war with america because there'd be no  chance of a trade treaty with america  and so boris johnson is going to end up  as the most  isolated prime minister in peacetime  history  with no friends around the world because  he has  simply chosen a path of confrontation  when everybody knows  that it's in britain's economic interest  maybe not in the  brexiteers ideological interests but  it's in britain's economic interest  to get a deal and to get a deal now so  hang on a minute though  if you're saying that no deal would put  us in an economic war with europe  and an economic war with america surely  you think that labour should back a  trade deal  well that's up to pierre starman to make  his decision but i would make my  decision and i don't think he's going to  make his decision until he sees what the  deal is  one of the problems that we've got  sophie  hold on one of the problems we got is  the difference between a no deal and a  deal  is becoming quite slim because the deal  that i see coming forward  and i you know i i want us to be in  an economic relationship with europe and  therefore i want a deal at this stage  but the deal that is coming forward is  quite a difficult one though  because most of the big questions about  state aids and about  what would happen if britain didn't  follow the level playing field  most of these questions may yet be  unresolved by the deal  it looks as if what's going to happen is  they're going to say yes we'll have tara  free trade we'll have quota free trade  but the minute you breach the level  playing field that we want to see happen  we are  reserving the right to impose tariffs  and impose  sanctions upon you and so you may have  an unstable relationship over the next  few months or even indeed over the next  few years because all they can get to in  this negotiation is a minimalist  deal which leaves people very uncertain  about the path of the economy  the chancellor has certainly got his  work cut out trying to negotiate the  economic impact of brexit and of course  as well the  economic crisis of covid at the same  time rishi sunak is one of the few  politicians who seems to have his  approval ratings intact some people say  he's got his eye on the top job  what advice would you give to a  chancellor who  might have his eye on moving from number  11 to number 10.  well you know i used to say when i was  the chancellor there was only two kinds  of chancellor  there were those who failed and those  who got out just in time  and if i were richie sunic i would think  that uh having given out all this money  uh his problems are really now beginning  and i think he's going to be tested and  i think he  i supported what he did in march and a  lot of us did i think he's  not done well in the last two months  with the measures he's taken they've  been  i think we've had five budget statements  and he has not provided for the recovery  in the way that  should be done and i think he faces huge  problems in the future and that will  decide his fate  is whether he's a good chancellor or not  not whether he wants to be prime  minister or not  and i think that he's making some  mistakes now that he should rectify  and i fear a number of things very high  unemployment  i think there's a million young people  who are not working at the moment and  that's a tragedy  for future generations that they cannot  get the chance  to show that they've got a skill and get  a job i suspect there are hundreds of  thousands of businesses that are just  being kept alive by loans  but they don't have the economic  activity that can guarantee the the  future  and you need to underpin the economy by  greater strength  from the treasury and from the bank of  england than i think is being provided  at the moment so  he has got his work cut out and he must  plan a recovery  and not rest on his laurels because  we've had a  rescue operation we need a recovery  operation now  and i don't think that is forthcoming  yet from the government

